  "notes": "USAGE:\nStrongly associated with Shinto purification practices. At shrines, visitors wash their hands and mouth at the temizuya ({手水舎|てみずや}) — this act is called {手|て}を{清|きよ}める. Sumo wrestlers throw salt to {清|きよ}める the ring before a bout.\n\nThe noun form {清|きよ}め refers to the act of purification itself.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {身|み}を{清|きよ}める: to purify oneself\n- {心|こころ}を{清|きよ}める: to purify one's heart\n- {手|て}を{清|きよ}める: to wash/purify one's hands (at a shrine)\n- {場|ば}を{清|きよ}める: to purify a place\n- {塩|しお}で{清|きよ}める: to purify with salt",
